  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 120 PM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThursday begins the second phase of this multi-day heavy rain event \nwith a vortmax rotating around the southeastern periphery of the H5 \nlow and over the Desert Southwest. This feature will act to push the \nmain area of fast moving SW-NE moving thunderstorm activity eastward \ntoward the Rio Grande Valley and central mountain chain of NM. \nRemaining in the warm sector of this storm system as well the \nsubtropical source region for the anomalous moisture plume, snow \nlevels will remain very high over NM. Much if not nearly all of this \nevent's precipitation will be in the form of rain, even over the \nnorthern high terrain. This is also resulting in a rain on snow \nthreat over the CO San Juans, in turn increasing the risk of higher \nriver flows along the San Juan basin of northwestern NM. Areas along \nriver banks will be susceptible to flooding in this area. \n\nOtherwise, Thursday and Thursday night into Friday morning sees the \nbulk of the heavy rainfall risk over western, central, and northern \nNM. Whether from fast moving storms repeatedly passing over the same \nlower to mid-level elevations or orographically enhanced \nprecipitation over the western and northern mountains, excessive \nrainfall threatening flash flooding will be a persistent concern \nthru this timeframe. Generally speaking, lower to mid-elevation \nareas of central and western NM look to receive 0.50\" to 0.75\" with \nlocally higher amounts upwards of 1.00+\". The higher terrain of the \nsouthwestern mountains, near the Continental Divide in west-central \nNM, and the Jemez and Tusas Mountains look to catch 1 to 2 inches \nwith locally higher amounts upwards of 2 to 4 inches. A marginal to \nslight risk of excessive rainfall was maintained by the WPC as a \nresult. Low lying and poorly drained areas, low water crossings, \nurban areas, and recent burn scars are most at risk. Timing favors \nareas along the Rio Grande Valley from Socorro to ABQ to Santa Fe to \nTaos Thursday night through Friday morning for the heaviest round(s) \nof thunderstorm rain. \n\nThe final phase of this event has seen numerical model guidance come \ninto better agreement with a secondary H5 low closing off over \nwestern AZ. This feature looks to continue tapping into subtropical \nmoisture, bringing it up into central and eastern NM late Friday and \ninto the weekend. Areas along and east of the central mountain chain \nwill then be favored for repeated rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms, increasing their own risk for flooding impacts. Areas \nalong and west of the Rio Grande Valley look to dry out during this \ntime, with one more round of showers/storms favored thru \nnorthwestern NM Saturday afternoon as the H5 low looks to track \nnortheastward into UT. Precipitation chances and intensities look to \nslowly dwindle for central and eastern NM Sunday and into next\nweek.",
